Patch summary:
The ColorBuffer staging buffer is made at its first use, at the size the
transfer needs (in 8 MiB steps), instead of 128 MiB of host-visible memory
at start. It grows when a larger transfer comes, up to the old 128 MiB
limit. Under WSL2 every byte of it was committed Windows memory for the
whole session; most hosts never copy more than one display-sized
ColorBuffer at once.
The staging memory is also treated as coherent only when its type is
HOST_COHERENT; the flag used to be set from HOST_VISIBLE, so non-coherent
staging memory was never flushed or invalidated.
DSA (desktop-subsystem-for-android) defers the same buffer in its patch 0026.
